Q: Bitwise operation not working as expected (C using Gameboy development kit) A: <p>If you want both numbers to be under <code>0x10</code>  (15 or less) then you have to shift one of them, as in</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>unsigned set_nibble(unsigned upper, unsigned lower)
{
    return ((upper &amp; 0x0f) &lt;&lt; 4) | (lower &amp; 0x0f);
}
</code></pre>
<p>The number returned, despite of being an <code>unsigned</code> is warranted to have only the lower eight bits different than zero.</p>

Q: Why declaration of unnamed struct causes compilation error A: <p><code>struct { ... };</code>  At the namespace scope is not allowed.</p>
<p>Class specifiers are allowed to omit the name, and that does define an unnamed class, but that does not mean the declaration is valid.</p>
<pre><code>struct {
   int hi;
   int bye;
};
</code></pre>
<p>The above is omitting the <code>init-declarator-list</code> , which C++ only allows for class/enum declarations, and then requires that the declaration still (re)introduce at least one name. With no name AND no declarators, it introduces zero names and so violates the rule and is ill-formed.</p>
<p>C++ does have a carve-out for anonymous unions, which do inject their members into the class scope, which is why anonymous unions are a thing but anonymous structs are not.</p>
<p>This isn't a grammar level rejection. The parser can build it, but the &quot;abstract declarator ... used as declaration&quot; gets lost in the machinery within the compiler and is rejected.</p>
<p>GCC encounters the above.</p>
<p>Clang is closer to the real intent, but doesn't go into the real details.</p>
<p>MSVC's &quot;empty declaration&quot; is oddly accurate, but isn't useful.</p>
<p>None of them are obligated to phrase the diagnostic nicely, they're only obligated to diagnose. Could this be better? Probably, yes. I personally think this type of usage would be bad practice in general. Maybe send some tickets to the compiler teams?</p>
<p>Either way this is explicitly forbidden by the C++ standard, and both anonymous enums and class are ill formed. You can typedef named structs, though, which I use occasionally.</p>

